1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,340
You get paid, and two weeks later

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:03,483
the money is just gone,

3
00:00:03,383 --> 00:00:05,173
and you're not sure where it went.

4
00:00:05,453 --> 00:00:07,393
One simple rule can fix that:

5
00:00:07,293 --> 00:00:08,789
fifty, thirty, twenty.

6
00:00:09,069 --> 00:00:10,732
By the end, you'll know your own

7
00:00:10,632 --> 00:00:12,392
three numbers, down to the dollar.

8
00:00:13,192 --> 00:00:14,333
The idea is simple.

9
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:16,962
You split your take-home pay into three buckets.

10
00:00:17,242 --> 00:00:18,846
Fifty percent goes to needs,

11
00:00:18,746 --> 00:00:21,047
the things you must pay to live and work.

12
00:00:21,327 --> 00:00:23,054
Thirty percent goes to wants,

13
00:00:22,954 --> 00:00:25,297
the fun stuff that makes life enjoyable.

14
00:00:25,577 --> 00:00:27,795
And twenty percent goes to your future:

15
00:00:27,695 --> 00:00:29,369
savings and paying down debt.

16
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:31,828
It was made popular by Elizabeth Warren

17
00:00:31,728 --> 00:00:33,161
and her daughter, Amelia,

18
00:00:33,061 --> 00:00:34,707
in their book All Your Worth.

19
00:00:35,507 --> 00:00:37,732
Step one: start with your take-home pay,

20
00:00:37,632 --> 00:00:38,582
not your salary.

21
00:00:38,862 --> 00:00:40,986
That's what actually lands in your bank

22
00:00:40,886 --> 00:00:42,024
account after taxes.

23
00:00:42,304 --> 00:00:44,240
If money for retirement comes out of

24
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:46,841
your paycheck, count it toward your twenty percent.

25
00:00:47,121 --> 00:00:48,358
Let's use an example:

26
00:00:48,258 --> 00:00:50,038
three thousand dollars a month.

27
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:52,283
With three thousand dollars,

28
00:00:52,183 --> 00:00:53,148
the math is quick.

29
00:00:53,428 --> 00:00:55,333
Fifteen hundred dollars for needs.

30
00:00:55,613 --> 00:00:57,330
Nine hundred dollars for wants.

31
00:00:57,610 --> 00:00:59,896
And six hundred dollars for savings and

32
00:00:59,796 --> 00:01:01,018
extra debt payments.

33
00:01:01,298 --> 00:01:02,841
That's it. Three numbers,

34
00:01:02,741 --> 00:01:04,399
and every dollar has a job.

35
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:07,120
Needs are the bills you'd still have

36
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:08,588
to pay, even in a hard month.

37
00:01:08,868 --> 00:01:10,770
Rent or mortgage, utilities,

38
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:12,058
and basic groceries.

39
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:14,435
Getting to work, like a car payment,

40
00:01:14,335 --> 00:01:15,490
gas, or a bus pass.

41
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:18,596
Insurance, child care, and the minimum payment

42
00:01:18,496 --> 00:01:19,426
on every debt.

43
00:01:19,706 --> 00:01:21,676
Notice: groceries are a need,

44
00:01:21,576 --> 00:01:22,901
but takeout is not.

45
00:01:23,701 --> 00:01:26,076
Wants are everything that's nice to have,

46
00:01:25,976 --> 00:01:27,019
but not required.

47
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:29,079
Eating out, streaming services,

48
00:01:28,979 --> 00:01:30,434
new clothes, and hobbies.

49
00:01:30,714 --> 00:01:33,576
Trips, concerts, and that upgrade to the newest phone.

50
00:01:33,856 --> 00:01:35,683
And here's the part people love:

51
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:38,059
this money is meant to be spent, guilt free.

52
00:01:38,339 --> 00:01:40,388
A budget you actually enjoy is a

53
00:01:40,288 --> 00:01:42,337
budget you'll actually stick to.

54
00:01:43,137 --> 00:01:45,518
The last twenty percent is for future you.

55
00:01:45,798 --> 00:01:48,036
First, build a small emergency fund,

56
00:01:47,936 --> 00:01:50,294
so surprises don't turn into new debt.

57
00:01:50,574 --> 00:01:52,838
Next, pay extra on high-interest debt,

58
00:01:52,738 --> 00:01:53,863
like credit cards.

59
00:01:54,143 --> 00:01:56,736
Then save for retirement and your bigger goals.

60
00:01:57,016 --> 00:01:59,626
Remember, minimum payments count as needs.

61
00:01:59,526 --> 00:02:01,599
Anything extra you pay goes here.

62
00:02:02,399 --> 00:02:03,833
Now the eye-opening part:

63
00:02:03,733 --> 00:02:06,180
compare the plan with what you really spend.

64
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:07,986
Pull up your last two or three

65
00:02:07,886 --> 00:02:09,648
months of bank and card statements.

66
00:02:09,928 --> 00:02:11,880
Sort each purchase into needs,

67
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:12,991
wants, or savings.

68
00:02:13,271 --> 00:02:14,979
Most people are surprised.

69
00:02:14,879 --> 00:02:17,699
Wants often hide in small, everyday charges.

70
00:02:18,499 --> 00:02:20,537
Here's the question people ask most:

71
00:02:20,437 --> 00:02:22,744
what if my needs take way more than half?

72
00:02:23,024 --> 00:02:25,695
That's really common, especially with high rent

73
00:02:25,595 --> 00:02:26,571
or a low income.

74
00:02:26,851 --> 00:02:28,381
It doesn't mean you failed.

75
00:02:28,281 --> 00:02:29,706
The numbers are a target,

76
00:02:29,606 --> 00:02:30,235
not a law.

77
00:02:30,515 --> 00:02:32,854
Try sixty, twenty, twenty for a while,

78
00:02:32,754 --> 00:02:34,563
or even seventy, twenty, ten.

79
00:02:34,843 --> 00:02:36,676
The one thing to protect is saving

80
00:02:36,576 --> 00:02:38,204
something, even if it's small.

81
00:02:39,004 --> 00:02:40,671
Then chip away at your needs,

82
00:02:40,571 --> 00:02:41,265
bit by bit.

83
00:02:41,545 --> 00:02:43,881
Shop around for car and renters insurance

84
00:02:43,781 --> 00:02:44,535
once a year.

85
00:02:44,815 --> 00:02:46,939
Look at a cheaper prepaid phone plan.

86
00:02:46,839 --> 00:02:49,180
Many cost far less than the big carriers.

87
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:50,522
Cook at home more,

88
00:02:50,422 --> 00:02:52,445
and buy store brands for the basics.

89
00:02:52,725 --> 00:02:54,066
And when your lease is up,

90
00:02:53,966 --> 00:02:56,072
think about a roommate or a cheaper place.

91
00:02:56,872 --> 00:02:59,153
The secret to sticking with it? Automate it.

92
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:01,493
On payday, move your twenty percent to

93
00:03:01,393 --> 00:03:03,144
savings before you can spend it.

94
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:04,857
Put your bills on autopay,

95
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:06,549
so your needs are always covered.

96
00:03:06,829 --> 00:03:08,728
Whatever is left in checking is your

97
00:03:08,628 --> 00:03:10,176
wants money. Spend it freely.

98
00:03:10,976 --> 00:03:12,759
A few mistakes trip people up,

99
00:03:12,659 --> 00:03:13,992
so let's fix them now.

100
00:03:14,272 --> 00:03:16,530
One: using your salary before taxes.

101
00:03:16,430 --> 00:03:18,208
Your numbers end up too big.

102
00:03:18,488 --> 00:03:20,329
Two: calling every want a need.

103
00:03:20,229 --> 00:03:21,678
Be honest with yourself.

104
00:03:21,958 --> 00:03:24,558
Three: forgetting costs that only come once

105
00:03:24,458 --> 00:03:25,488
or twice a year,

106
00:03:25,388 --> 00:03:27,058
like car repairs and gifts.

107
00:03:27,338 --> 00:03:28,728
Divide those by twelve,

108
00:03:28,628 --> 00:03:30,858
and set that amount aside every month.

109
00:03:31,658 --> 00:03:33,953
Let's recap the whole plan in four steps.

110
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:35,595
Find your take-home pay.

111
00:03:35,875 --> 00:03:37,680
Split it fifty, thirty, twenty.

112
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:39,999
Compare it with your real spending,

113
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:41,605
and adjust if needs run high.

114
00:03:41,885 --> 00:03:43,264
And automate your savings,

115
00:03:43,164 --> 00:03:44,787
so it happens without thinking.

116
00:03:45,587 --> 00:03:47,270
You don't need a fancy app or

117
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:49,671
a finance degree. Just three simple numbers.

118
00:03:49,951 --> 00:03:52,369
If your twenty percent is starting from

119
00:03:52,269 --> 00:03:54,865
zero, watch our emergency fund video next.

120
00:03:55,145 --> 00:03:57,140
If this helped, like the video and

121
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:59,537
subscribe for a simple money tip every day.

122
00:03:59,817 --> 00:04:01,043
Tell me in the comments:

123
00:04:00,943 --> 00:04:02,356
what are your three numbers?

124
00:04:02,256 --> 00:04:03,482
See you in the next one.
